# Typography

Typography is a primary part of the interface.

Do not treat it as decoration.

## Hierarchy

Establish clear levels for:

- display
- heading
- subheading
- body
- metadata
- labels
- captions

Do not create arbitrary font sizes for every element.

Use the coherent type scale in `web/src/styles/global.css` (`text-xs` through `text-9xl` via `--font-scale`).

## Font choices

Respect the existing brand/system fonts (Plus Jakarta Sans Variable + CJK-safe fallbacks).

Do not introduce a new font without a reason.
Do not load full CJK web fonts.

## Weight

Use weight intentionally.

Do not make everything bold.

## Line height

Body text should prioritize comfortable reading.

Headings may use tighter line height when appropriate.

## Measure

Avoid extremely wide text blocks.

Long-form content should use a readable line length.

## Contrast

Text hierarchy should come from a combination of:

- size
- weight
- spacing
- color
- position

not simply making everything larger.

## Numbers

For metrics, prices, dates, and tabular values:

- align numbers appropriately
- preserve readable precision
- avoid unnecessary decimals
- use consistent formatting

## UI Copy

Typography includes the words displayed to users, not just visual styling.

Prefer clear, complete, and human-readable wording.

Avoid shortened words, truncated labels, or informal abbreviations when space is not genuinely limited.

Prefer:

- "Settings" instead of "Prefs"
- "Notifications" instead of "Notifs"
- "Information" instead of "Info"
- "Configuration" instead of "Config"
- "Application" instead of "App" (unless the product officially uses "App")
- "Telephone Number" or "Phone Number" instead of "Phone No."
- "Quantity" instead of "Qty"
- "Maximum" instead of "Max"
- "Minimum" instead of "Min"

Use complete sentences for helper text, descriptions, validation messages, and empty states.

Avoid vague or clipped labels that require users to guess their meaning.

## Acceptable Abbreviations

Use abbreviations only when they are:

- official product terminology
- widely recognized technical standards
- industry-standard units or symbols
- necessary because of genuine space constraints

Examples include:

- API
- URL
- PDF
- CSV
- HTML
- CSS
- JSON
- HTTP
- HTTPS
- ECM
- SAP
- CMS
- SEO
- WCAG

Do not invent new abbreviations or shorten words for stylistic reasons.

## Consistency

Use the same terminology throughout the product.

Avoid mixing abbreviated and full versions of the same term.

Example:

✓ "Notifications" everywhere

✗ "Notifications" on one page and "Notifs" on another

Consistency improves readability, professionalism, and user confidence.
